<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julio Cortázar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1984">1984</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julio Cortázar was an Argentine writer who lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from 1951 and became a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American Boom. He is best known for his experimental novel Hopscotch (1963), which can be read in more than one order, and for short stories such as 'Blow-Up', which inspired the film by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> Antonioni.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julio-cortazar.jpg" alt="Julio Cortázar" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sara Facio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cort%C3%A1zar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
